<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atbara River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atbara is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, about 800 kilometres long, which rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> at the town of Atbara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, the Nile's last tributary. It floods in summer and shrinks to pools in the dry season. The Battle of Atbara was fought on its banks in 1898 during the Anglo-Egyptian reconquest of Sudan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atbara-river.jpg" alt="Atbara River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shihab E. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_Atbara_and_Setit_Dam_Complex_Power_Plant.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
